  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Current analysis this morning depicts a complex\nmid-level pattern with three distinct shortwaves digging across\nportions of the Great Lakes, Midwest and MS Valley while primary\njet axis sits just to their east over the spine of the\nAppalachians. At the surface, main feature of interest for us \nis a stalled to slow moving front sitting across far western NC\nwhich is just now beginning to push eastward. Pre-frontal \ntrough ahead of it is driving a broken band of showers and a few\nembedded thunderstorms extending roughly along the I-85 \ncorridor.\n\nThe forecast for today has changed little, with widespread\nshowers and a few thunderstorms developing late this afternoon\nas the aforementioned front finally makes its way through our\narea. Most CAMs continue to depict some isolated showers\nmigrating NEward off the water along the pre-frontal trough.\nThis is certainly possible although it is worth noting this is a\ncommon artifact in guidance that has a very mixed history of\nbeing realized. If such activity does develop, it could modestly\nhamper destabilization through the day. In either scenario, a\nminimum of around 500 J/kg MLCAPE (worst case scenario 1000 J/kg\nas suggested by the HRRR) to be in place this afternoon across \nthe region fueled by above average moisture and temperatures in \nthe mid to upper 70s. This is unusually high for early November,\nsupported by EFI highlighting CAPE values in the 75-80th \npercentile.\n\nIn addition to instability, respectable low-level shear will be\npresent ahead of the front with 0-1 km values potentially\nreaching 20-25 kt. This will support a pre-storm environment\nthat could support organized convection and possibly low-topped\nsupercells in the afternoon and evening hours. The primary\nhazard would be damaging winds, but model hodographs do show\nlow-level veering suggesting a brief tornado is not implausible.\n\nThe main threat window runs from early afternoon to around\nsunset inland, lingering slightly longer over the Outer Banks\nbut with a diminishing severe risk as instability collapses. The\nmain front sweeps offshore late tonight with any remaining \nshowers exiting the OBX around or shortly after midnight. There\nwill likely be a delay between the main front coming through and\nthe dry air surge, so would not be surprised to see a period of\nlow stratus Fri night into Sat morning.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A more seasonably dry airmass will be ushered \nin for the weekend behind the front. Expect a return to mostly \nsunny skies and highs in the upper 60s to low 70s Sat, then \ncooler yet for Sun with 60-65. Lows will be in the 40s, with \nsome 30s for some of the normally colder spots. We will have to \nmonitor the temp forecast for any frost concerns Sunday morning.",
